homelab-backup-server (0.4.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: `setup` could stall invisibly forever right after "Checking for
    an existing admin login..." -- caught live. That check spawns
    `homelab-cli validate` with capture_output=True but no stdin
    redirect, so it inherits this script's REAL terminal as its own
    stdin; since root's ~/.config/homelab-cli almost never has an
    api_url configured, homelab-cli fell into its interactive prompt --
    but that prompt's text landed in the captured (invisible) output
    pipe while it kept blocking on the real, visible terminal for a
    line the operator had no way to know was being asked for. Every
    homelab-cli subprocess call here now explicitly redirects stdin from
    /dev/null (calls that already pass input= were unaffected). The
    ambient admin-login check specifically also gets a 3-second timeout
    now, since it's a best-effort probe, not the real work -- a slow or
    unresponsive homelab-api there just falls straight through to the
    explicit admin-login prompt instead of stalling setup.
  * `setup` now prints the homelab-api URL it resolved and is using,
    up front and again right before the admin-login prompt (which
    checks a *different*, ambient session that may point elsewhere) --
    an operator asked where the tool was even checking against.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:20:00 -0500

homelab-backup-server (0.4.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Renamed "service account"/"control-plane account" to "backup
    account" throughout `setup`'s prompts, messages, and --help text --
    an operator flagged that "service account" was genuinely ambiguous,
    since a homelab-api box can have several (dovecot, drive, backup,
    ...); this is specifically the one homelab-api account holding the
    `backup` role, used by this server and its clients.
  * Reordered/clarified the account-bootstrap flow. Previously, the
    first attempt silently tried the operator's own ambient homelab-cli
    session and only explained anything on failure -- but since `setup`
    runs as root via sudo, that ambient session almost never exists, so
    this was nearly always a silent failed step before the real admin
    prompt. Now: checks for an ambient admin login up front and reports
    what it found (or didn't) before attempting anything; if an ambient
    session exists, `validate`'s roles are used to print a precise
    message when it's rejected ("...failed while logged in as X (roles:
    user) -- this usually needs site_admin...") instead of a bare API
    error. homelab-api itself remains the sole authority on whether an
    account can create others -- the roles fetched here are only ever
    used for narration, never to block an attempt client-side.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:00:00 -0500

homelab-backup-server (0.4.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: a Ctrl-C during `setup` (e.g. mid-subprocess while creating/
    logging in the service account) surfaced as a raw Python traceback
    instead of a clean interrupt -- caught live. Same fix already
    applied to homelab-backup-client: caught at the top level, printing
    "interrupted" and exiting 130. Running out of piped input entirely
    (EOFError, e.g. a scripted invocation with too few answers) gets the
    same clean-message treatment instead of its own traceback.
  * Reworded the first `setup` prompt, which was genuinely ambiguous in
    practice: an operator typed their own personal admin email there,
    expecting it to be their own login, when it's actually asking what
    identity the SERVER ITSELF should use going forward (almost always
    the same shared account across the whole backup fleet, not a
    personal one). Now explains this upfront, before the prompt, and
    explicitly distinguishes it from the separate later prompt that
    really does want the operator's own admin credentials.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 12:30:00 -0500

homelab-backup-server (0.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: `setup` crashed with "config.yml: key not found and parent path
    missing: homelab_cli.email" on any host whose config.yml was
    migrated forward from the pre-redesign package -- that old config
    never had a homelab_cli:/reconcile: block at all, and the config
    editor could only insert a key under an EXISTING parent section, not
    create a missing one. Caught live on a real host. It now appends a
    brand-new top-level section when needed instead of failing outright.
  * Reworded the interactive `setup` flow away from "control-plane
    account"/"control-plane session" jargon toward plain language
    ("logged in to homelab-api as X", "this server's homelab-api login
    email") -- also drops a stale reference to an internal planning
    document's rollout step from one error message. `setup` now also
    prints a short explanation up front: this account doesn't need to
    exist ahead of time, and if it's missing you'll be asked once for an
    ADMIN homelab-api account to create it, never again after that.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 11:15:00 -0500

homelab-backup-server (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BREAKING: renamed from homelab-borg-service-backup-server to
    homelab-backup-server (binary, package, config dir
    /etc/homelab/homelab-borg-service-backup-server ->
    /etc/homelab/backup/server, bash completion, man page). postinst
    migrates an existing deployment's config dir forward automatically
    on upgrade (old copy left in place as a rollback breadcrumb; the
    borg repository itself is never touched).
  * New `homelab-api` control-plane integration: client hosts now
    enroll themselves via `homelab-cli backup enroll` instead of an
    administrator running `enroll` here by hand for every host. This
    server polls for pending enroll/revoke requests via the new
    `reconcile` subcommand, run automatically by the new
    homelab-backup-server-reconcile.timer (default every 15 seconds --
    the poll itself is a single cheap local API call, so there's no
    real cost to polling often; the low default exists specifically so
    a freshly-enrolled client isn't left waiting on a slow timer before
    its first backup. Configurable via config.yml's
    reconcile.interval_seconds, though changing it there alone doesn't
    take effect until 'setup' is re-run or the installed timer unit is
    reloaded -- see the comment in config.yml).
    `reconcile` reuses the exact same authorized_keys-building/parsing
    functions `enroll`/`revoke` always have, now writing
    authorized_keys via write-to-temp-file + os.rename() for true
    atomicity since the file is touched unattended on a timer.
    `reconcile` fails soft (WARNING + exit 0, authorized_keys untouched)
    on any control-plane error so an API outage can never disable the
    timer via systemd's failure-count limits.
  * New interactive `setup` subcommand: connects this server to the
    homelab-api control plane (bootstrapping its own service account if
    needed), registers this host as the backup server via `homelab-cli
    backup set-server-info`, and installs/enables the reconcile timer.
    `postinst` stays non-interactive/skeleton-only, same division of
    labor homelab-backup-client already uses between postinst and its
    own setup.
  * New hard dependency on homelab-cli (the reconcile job and `setup`
    both shell out to it). Added Replaces:/Breaks: against the old
    package name; Conflicts: borgbackup2 kept as-is.
  * `enroll`/`list`/`revoke`/`admin-recovery-key` are unchanged and
    remain available for manual/emergency use even with the
    reconciliation job in place.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 11:00:00 -0500

homelab-borg-service-backup-server (0.2.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: the borgbackup system user was created with --shell
    /usr/sbin/nologin, which silently broke every SSH forced command
    for this account regardless of correct enrollment -- OpenSSH runs
    an authorized_keys command="..." entry via the user's shell
    ($SHELL -c "command"), and nologin unconditionally refuses to run
    anything at all ("This account is currently not available."),
    ignoring the command entirely. Caught live: no borg serve session
    for this user could ever have succeeded. Now uses /bin/sh --
    command="..."/restrict in authorized_keys already fully constrains
    the connection, so nologin wasn't adding any extra restriction,
    only breaking the one thing this account exists for. Existing
    installs get their shell corrected automatically on upgrade.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 19:30:00 -0500

